5. Key findings

The examples show why a reaction name alone is not enough to complete a reliable pharmacovigilance assessment. Hospitalization is a seriousness criterion when it is due to the event; laboratory abnormalities need clinical context; and expectedness requires comparison with the relevant reference safety information. Missing details should be documented rather than guessed.

6. Conclusion

A structured assessment distinguishes seriousness from severity and verifies expectedness against an appropriate product reference. Complete source information, clear rationale, and documentation of uncertainty are essential to consistent case classification and patient-safety decision-making.
